effect at one or more commands (a non-literal `set` argument, or paths that
disagree), so it plans as though each might abort the book on failure -- the
cautious reading; it can cost elisions, never correctness.
loading: warning[script-relative-load-dies-slashless]: [unwritten:
script-relative-load-dies-slashless]
      [ book.sh:9:1
   9 | . "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"
     | \______________________/
loading: warning[slashless-source-searches-path]: [unwritten:
slashless-source-searches-path]
      [ book.sh:14:1
   14 | . wombat-helpers.sh
      | \_________________/
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=f6069b6b4689c0f233e511602bb7218cc57abba7fffd4a384a83cb222b39ae40 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 1 is a command-substitution `$(...)`
/ arithmetic / operator-form expansion, so Dorc cannot know its value until the
command runs on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe
to check. Dorc elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is
already in place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:9:3
   9 | . "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"
     |   \____________________/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 3 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `. "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"`, `. wombat-helpers.sh`, `hork tune
web` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to
stay safe)
      [ book.sh:9:1
   9 | . "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"
     | \______________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"`
why: ran because operand 1 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 9:1 `. "${0%/*}/hork.dorc.sh"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: '.' (line 9) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 2 more unmodeled walls -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

